=> Install WSL: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/wsl/install

=> What happens when we run: wsl --install

    -> Step 1: Windows checks whether CPU virtualization support is available.
       Example:
       - Intel VT-x
       - AMD-V

    -> Step 2: Windows enables the "Windows Subsystem for Linux (WSL)" feature.

    -> Step 3: Windows enables the "Virtual Machine Platform" feature.

    -> Step 4: Windows enables and starts the Microsoft Hypervisor.
       Note:
       - Hypervisor = Software
       - Intel VT-x / AMD-V = Hardware support used by Hypervisor

    -> Step 5: Windows downloads and installs the Linux Kernel required by WSL2.

    -> Step 6: Windows downloads and installs a Linux Distribution.
       Default:
       - Ubuntu

    -> Step 7: Windows configures WSL2 as the default WSL version.

    -> Step 8: Windows asks for a restart (if required).

    -> Step 9: After restart, Hypervisor creates a lightweight WSL2 Virtual Machine.

    -> Step 10: Linux Kernel boots inside the WSL2 Virtual Machine.

    -> Step 11: Ubuntu User Space starts on top of the Linux Kernel.

=> Initial Architecture

    Hardware
        ↓
    Intel VT-x / AMD-V
        ↓
    Microsoft Hypervisor
        ↓
    WSL2 Lightweight VM
        ↓
    Linux Kernel
        ↓
    Ubuntu User Space
        ↓
    Bash / Java / Python / Applications

=> If later we install another Linux Distribution (Debian/Kali/openSUSE)

    Hardware
        ↓
    Microsoft Hypervisor
        ↓
    WSL2 Lightweight VM
        ↓
    Shared Linux Kernel
        ├── Ubuntu User Space
        ├── Debian User Space
        ├── Kali User Space
        └── openSUSE User Space

    Note:
    - All distributions share the same Linux Kernel.
    - Each distribution has its own filesystem, packages and applications.
    - New Kernel is NOT downloaded for every distribution.

=> If later we install Docker Desktop

    Hardware
        ↓
    Microsoft Hypervisor
        ↓
    WSL2 Lightweight VM
        ↓
    Linux Kernel
        ↓
    Docker Engine
        ↓
    Containers

    Example:
    - Nginx Container
    - MySQL Container
    - Spring Boot Container
    - Redis Container

=> Docker Architecture with Ubuntu

    Hardware
        ↓
    Microsoft Hypervisor
        ↓
    WSL2 Lightweight VM
        ↓
    Linux Kernel
        ├── Ubuntu User Space
        │      └── Bash / Java / Python
        │
        └── Docker Engine
               ├── Nginx Container
               ├── MySQL Container
               ├── Redis Container
               └── Spring Boot Container

=> Important Notes

    -> Ubuntu is NOT a separate VM.

    -> Ubuntu does NOT have its own Kernel.

    -> Ubuntu uses the Linux Kernel downloaded by WSL2.

    -> Debian, Kali, Ubuntu, openSUSE all share the same Linux Kernel.

    -> Docker Containers also use the same Linux Kernel.

    -> Hypervisor creates the WSL2 VM only once.

    -> Linux Distributions and Docker run on top of that WSL2 environment.
